====================================================================================================
4. WHAT A REJECTION SAMPLER WOULD HAVE COST THE ARGUMENT
====================================================================================================
The code does NOT reject: Fp::from_u128 is `v % P` (field.rs:37),
so fp() consumes exactly 16 bytes whatever it draws.  The question
is what the argument would have needed if it did, because the
independence clause is currently justified by DISJOINT FIXED
POSITIONS, and rejection makes the positions random.

Exact model.  Stream of i.i.d. uniform symbols on {0,1,2,3}.  A
'draw' rejects the symbol 3 and retries; the value is the accepted
symbol.  Two draws, then one 'salt' symbol.  Everything exact.

  enumerated mass (truncated at depth 14) = 268435413/268435456 = 1 - 43/268435456
  salt marginal (should be mass/4 each): [Fraction(268435413, 1073741824)]
  max |joint(v,s) - P(v)*1/4| over all (v,s) = 0

  -> with rejection the salt is STILL exactly uniform and STILL
     exactly independent of the accepted values -- but NOT because
     its position is fixed: its position is the random stopping
     time T = (number of symbols consumed).  The reason is the
     STRONG MARKOV property of an i.i.d. stream: for a stopping
     time T of the stream filtration, the post-T stream is i.i.d.
     uniform and independent of F_T.

  Two consequences for the memo, and they point opposite ways:
   (a) the CONCLUSION would survive rejection sampling;
   (b) the ARGUMENT would not -- 'disjoint fixed positions' would
       have to be replaced by a stopping-time argument, and the
       stopping time would have to be shown WITNESS-FREE, which is
       a strictly stronger reading of the draw-accounting test.
  Since fp() reduces rather than rejects, the project needs only
  the weaker reading -- and that is a fact of `field.rs:37`, which
  no test pins as a privacy-relevant property.

ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED.
